#TODO(Explain why we introduce an ordering in rigor)

We can consider two different kind of orderings. One is a generalization of
$<=$ on real numbers, and the other is the one of $<$ on real numbers.

useenv defin [Ordering] {
    Let $X$ be a set. A relation $R$ in $X$ is an \it{(partial)
    ordering}\index{ordering!partial} if it satisfies the following
    properties.
    useenv enumerate [label=(\roman*)] {
        \item $a R a$ for every $a\in X$.
        \item For $a,b\in X$, if $a R b$ and $b R a$, then $a = b$.
        \item For $a,b,c\in X$, if $a R b$ and $b R c$, then $a R c$.
    }
    Then we say $(X,R)$ or simply $X$ an \it{ordered set}\index{set!ordered}.
}

useenv defin [Strict Ordering] {
    Let $X$ be a set. A relation $S$ in $X$ is a \it{strict
    ordering}\index{ordering!strict} if it satisfies the following conditions.

    useenv enumerate [label=(\roman*)] {
        \item If $a S b$ holds, then $b S a$ is not true.
        \item For $a,b,c\in X$, if $a S b$ and $b S c$, then $a S c$.
    }
}

For example, one can easily check that $<=$ is an order in the real number
system, and $<$ is a strict order in the real number system.
Notice that $a <= b$ holds if and only if either $a = b$ or $a < b$ holds.
On the other hand, $a < b$ if and only if $a <= b$ and $a != b$.
Those relations also true for orderings and strict orderings.
The following proposition is Theorem 2.5.6 in \cite{set-theory}.

#label(prop:ordering-and-strict-order)
useenv prop {
    #enum ((\alph*)) {
        \item Let $R$ be an ordering of $X$. Then the relation $S$ defined in
        $X$ by
        $$
            aSb \quad "if and only if"\quad aRb #"and"# a != b
        $$
        is a strict order of $A$.
        \item Let $S$ be a strict order of $X$. Then the relation $R$ defined in
        $X$ by
        $$
            aRb \quad "if and only if"\quad aSb #"or"# a = b
        $$
        is an ordering of $X$.
    }
    \proof
    See the proof of Theorem 2.5.6 in \cite{set-theory}. \proved
}

#label(remark:ordering-and-strict-order)
useenv remark {
    Throughout of this book, we will use a symbol $<=$ as an order on $X$, and
    $<$ a strict order on $X$, in general.
    In addition, if one defines an order $<=$, then $<$ is always understood in
    the sense of \Cref{prop:ordering-and-strict-order}, and vice versa.
}

useenv defin [Total Ordering, Well Ordering] {
    If an ordering is called by \it{totally ordered}\index{ordering!totally
    ordered} if
    useenv enumerate [label=(\roman*)] {
        \setcounter{enumi}{3}
        \item For $a,b\in X$, either $a < b$, $a = b$, or $a > b$ is satisfied.
    }
    In this case, we say that an ordered set as a \it{totally ordered
    set}.\index{set!totally ordered}

    Finally, if a totally ordered set has a property that every nonempty set has
    the minimum element, we say that the totally ordered set is \it{well
    ordered}\index{set!well ordered}.
}

useenv defin {
    Let $(X,<=)$ be an ordered set. Then we define
    \vskip1em
    %-
    \hbox to\textwidth{\hss\vbox{\halign{&\tabskip=3pt$#$&$\defeq$#&$#$\tabskip=0pt&#\cr
        (a,b) && \{x : a < x < b\} &,\kern2em&
        [a,b] && \{x : a \leq x \leq b\}&,\cr
        \noalign{\vskip0.3em}
        (a,b] && \{x : a < x \leq b\} &,\kern2em&
        [a,b) && \{x : a \leq x < b\} &.\cr
    }}\hss}
    -%
    \vskip1em
    All of four sets are called an \it{interval}\index{interval}.
    Especially, $(a,b)$ is called an \it{open inverval}\index{interval!open} and
    $[a,b]$ is called a \it{closed interval}\index{interval!closed}.
}
